    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In Genesis, God calls Abram to leave his father's house for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and promises him descendants and land; he is later renamed Abraham, meaning father of a multitude.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> hold him as the ancestor of the covenant, Christians as the spiritual father of believers, and Muslims as a prophet and the first to proclaim the oneness of God.</li><li>Most historians no longer regard the patriarchal narratives as historical, and many believe the Abraham traditions developed orally before entering Genesis in the Persian period.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> Abraham is mentioned in 35 chapters, more often than any other biblical personage apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

