    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">Leaving Ur</span><span class="rf-text">Terah's family leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> of the Chaldees for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and settles in Haran</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">The call</span><span class="rf-text">At 75 Abram leaves Haran with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarai</a> and Lot and travels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">The rescue of Lot</span><span class="rf-text">He rescues Lot from the Elamite army and is blessed by Melchizedek</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Ishmael</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagar">Hagar</a> bears <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishmael">Ishmael</a> when Abram is 86</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">The covenant</span><span class="rf-text">At 99 he is renamed Abraham and receives the covenant of circumcision</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Isaac</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac">Isaac</a> is born and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagar">Hagar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishmael">Ishmael</a> are sent away</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">The binding of Isaac</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binding-of-isaac">binding of Isaac</a> in the land of Moriah</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">His death</span><span class="rf-text">He dies at 175 and is buried in the cave of Machpelah</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sarah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarah.jpg" alt="Sarah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Jacques_Joseph_Tissot_-_Abram's_Counsel_to_Sarai_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Jacques Joseph Tissot (1836 - 1902) (French) Details on Google Art Project, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> introduces Abram, later called Abraham, as the son of Terah, ninth in descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a>, and brother of Nahor and Haran. Haran, the father of Lot, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> of the Chaldees while Terah was alive. Abram married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarah</a>, called Sarai at first, but unlike Nahor and his wife Milcah they were childless. Genesis records that Terah, Abram, Sarai and Lot set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> but settled at a place named Haran, where Terah died at 205. According to some exegetes, such as Nahmanides, Abram was actually born in Haran and later moved to Ur.</p><p>Scholars treat this world differently from the text. In the early and middle 20th century scholars such as William F. Albright and John Bright believed the patriarchs were real individuals or composites who lived in the 2nd millennium BCE. In the 1970s Thomas L. Thompson and John Van Seters argued that the stories reflect conditions of the 1st millennium BCE, and most scholars now see the patriarchal age as a late literary construct, though the stories may retain some historical qualities. Some scholars, among them Daniel Fleming, see parallels with the Amorite culture attested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and Alan Millard holds that the name Abram is Amorite.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Abraham&#8221; cites where it covers Origins of the narrative:</p><ul><li>Jean Louis Ska, The Exegesis of the Pentateuch: Exegetical Studies and Basic Questions (Mohr Siebeck, 2009)</li><li>Jean Louis Ska, Introduction to Reading the Pentateuch (Eisenbrauns, 2006)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/hagar">Hagar<span>Married</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarah<span>Married</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/isaac">Isaac<span>Child</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/ishmael">Ishmael<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shechem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shechem.jpg" alt="Shechem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tell_Balata.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TrickyH, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In Genesis 12, God tells Abram to leave his country and kindred for a land that God will show him, promising to make of him a great nation, to bless him and to make his name great. Abram was 75 when he left Haran with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarai</a>, his nephew Lot and their people, and he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, where he built an altar, and then to a place between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a> and Ai. When a famine struck Canaan he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he told Sarai to say she was his sister, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> took her into his palace until plagues afflicted his household and he sent the couple away.</p><p>Abram and Lot, whose herds had grown too large for the same pastures, separated. Lot settled in the plain of the Jordan near Sodom and Abram went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, at the plain of Mamre. When Elamite forces took Lot captive, Abram gathered 318 trained servants, pursued them north and rescued Lot by a night raid. On his return Melchizedek, king of Salem and priest of El Elyon, brought him bread and wine and blessed him, and Abram gave him a tenth of everything. In a vision, God again promised him a son and descendants as numerous as the stars, and made a covenant with him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hagar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagar.jpg" alt="Hagar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hagar">Hagar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:François-Joseph_Navez_-_Agar_et_Ismaël_dans_le_désert_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">François-Joseph Navez, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After ten years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarai</a> gave her Egyptian slave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagar">Hagar</a> to Abram, and Hagar bore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishmael">Ishmael</a> when Abram was 86. When Abram was 99, God appeared again, renamed him Abraham, meaning father of a multitude, renamed Sarai as Sarah, and gave him the covenant of circumcision, which Abraham performed that day on every male of his household, himself and Ishmael included. Not long after, three visitors came to his tent at Mamre and told him Sarah would have a son in the following year; Sarah laughed. The visitors went on to judge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sodom-and-gomorrah">Sodom and Gomorrah</a>, and Abraham pleaded with God that the cities be spared if ten righteous people were found there.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac">Isaac</a> was born when Abraham was "an hundred years old", and he was circumcised at eight days. Sarah then told Abraham to send away Hagar and Ishmael, and Abraham did so after God told him that Ishmael would also be the father of a nation. At some point in Isaac's youth, God commanded Abraham to offer Isaac as a sacrifice in the land of Moriah; an angel stopped him at the last moment and a ram was sacrificed in the son's place. Abraham made agreements with King Abimelech at Gerar and Beersheba. When Sarah died he bought the cave of Machpelah near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a> from Ephron the Hittite as her tomb. He took another wife, Keturah, and had six more sons.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/torah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torah.jpg" alt="Torah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Тора._Нацыянальная_бібліятэка_Беларусі.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Genesis stories do not name an enemy of Abraham. His trials come as crises that threaten the promise: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarah</a>'s childlessness, the famine, the capture of Lot, and the command to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac">Isaac</a>. The narrative also contains episodes that readers have found hard. Abraham presents Sarah twice as his sister, once to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> and once to Abimelech, who was warned in a dream and returned her; when Abimelech asked why, Abraham said he thought there was no fear of God in that place and added that she was in fact his father's daughter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagar">Hagar</a> fled into the wilderness after Sarai mistreated her, and was later sent away with her son.</p><p>Later biblical texts show the tradition being contested. In the Book of Ezekiel, written during the Exile, those who stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> claim the land by inheritance from Abraham, and the prophet answers that they have no claim because they do not observe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>. The Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> says Israel's history begins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> rather than with Abraham. Scholars infer that Abraham was a figure used by the landowners of Judah to support their claims against the returning exiles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein">Israel Finkelstein</a> and Thomas Romer suggest that the oldest traditions were an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hero story and that he may have been worshipped as an ancestor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cave-of-the-patriarchs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cave-of-the-patriarchs.jpg" alt="Cave of the Patriarchs" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cave-of-the-patriarchs">Cave of the Patriarchs</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hebron_Cave_of_the_Patriarchs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Utilisateur:Djampa - Djampa, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to Genesis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarah</a> died and Abraham buried her in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cave-of-the-patriarchs">Cave of the Patriarchs</a>, the cave of Machpelah near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, which he had bought with the adjoining field from Ephron the Hittite. The purchase is told as establishing his right to the land. After her death he took Keturah, a concubine, and had six sons with her: Zimran, Jokshan, Medan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midian">Midian</a>, Ishbak and Shuah. He lived to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac">Isaac</a> marry Rebekah, a woman from his own kin, and the birth of his twin grandsons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob">Jacob</a> and Esau.</p><p>Abraham died at the age of 175 and was buried in the cave of Machpelah, beside Sarah, by his sons Isaac and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishmael">Ishmael</a>. On his death Isaac received all Abraham's goods, while the other sons received only gifts. The promise of land and descendants passes to Isaac, while Ishmael is promised that he will be the founder of a great nation. In the Genesis account the tomb at Hebron remains the family burial place. Jewish tradition counts Abraham with Isaac and Jacob as one of the three whose names are joined to God in the phrase God of Abraham, God of Isaac and God of Jacob. Genesis also lists the nations that descend from him: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, Ishmaelites, Edomites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalekites">Amalekites</a>, Kenizzites and Midianites, with the Moabites and Ammonites related through Lot.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kaaba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaaba.jpg" alt="Kaaba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ka%27ba,_Great_Mosque_of_Mecca,_Saudi_Arabia_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> Abraham is Avraham Avinu, our father Abraham, the ancestor of the Jewish people and the one with whom the covenant began, and his story is read in the weekly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> portions Lech-Lecha, Vayeira, Chayei <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarah">Sarah</a> and Toledot. Jewish legend tells that he broke the idols of the Chaldeans and was saved from Nimrod's furnace. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> Paul taught that Abraham's faith, which came before the Mosaic law, made him the prototype of all believers, Jewish or gentile; Augustine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> later called him the spiritual father of believers. The Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> calls him our father in faith, and several churches keep his feast days.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> Ibrahim is a prophet in the chain from Adam to Muhammad through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishmael">Ishmael</a>, a hanif and a muslim, the Friend of God and the reformer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, whose mission was to proclaim the oneness of God. Muslims also call him the father of Muslims. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> count him as the third spokesman after Adam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a>, Baha'is as a Manifestation of God, and Mandaeans as an original Mandaean priest. He is regarded as the common forefather of the Arab people through Ishmael and of the Jewish people through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac">Isaac</a>. Artists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a> and Marc Chagall have returned to the sacrifice of Isaac, and Kierkegaard's Fear and Trembling (1843) takes it as its subject.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Abraham is the patriarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, called by God to leave Haran for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians and Muslims each revere him, as ancestor of the covenant, father of believers and prophet. Most scholars see the narratives as late literary traditions.</em></p>

